timertt (Timer Thread Template) is a small, template based, header only library
for C++11. It implements timer threads: thread which handles timers. timertt
has no external dependecies except standard C++11 library.

timertt was developed as part of SObjectizer[1] project but can be used as
standalone library.

timertt is distributed under 3-clauses BSD license (see LICENSE file).

Obtaining and Using
===================

There are three methods for obtaining timertt library source code.

1. Download minimal timertt archive from SObjectizer files section on
SourceForge[2]. This archive has name like timertt-1.1.X-headeronly.7z. Just
unpack this archive at appropriate location and add timertt folder to your
project’s INCLUDE path.

2. Download full timertt archive from SObjectizer files section on
SourceForge[2]. This archive has name like timertt-1.1.X-full.7z. Just unpack
this archive at appropriate location and add timertt-1.1.X/dev/timertt folder
to your project’s INCLUDE path.

Ruby and Mxx_ru tool are necessary for building timertt tests and samples.
Mxx_ru can be installed as RubyGem by the command:

> gem install Mxx_ru

To build timertt tests and samples go to the timertt-1.1.X/dev folder and run:

> ruby build_all.rb

3. Export (or check out) timertt source code from Subversion repository on
SourceForge. For example:

> svn export http://svn.code.sf.net/p/sobjectizer/repo/tags/timertt/1.1.1 timertt-1.1.1

Then add timertt-1.1.X to your project's INCLUDE path.

Like in previous method Ruby and Mxx_ru are necessary for building timertt
tests and samples.

API Reference manual can be built by Doxygen tool:

> cd timertt-1.1.X/doxygen
> doxygen

Generated documenation will be stored into timertt-1.1.X/dev/doc/html folder.

Main Project Documentation
==========================

Main documenation for timertt library is on SObjectizer’s wiki[3] on
SourceForge.

Bug Reporting and Feedback
==========================

For bug reporting, proposals, discussions and stuff like that the appropriate
SObjectizer’s forums on SourceForge must be used [4],[5],[6]

